  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 103 PM EDT SUN OCT 4 2026\n\nAs the long term period opens, a vertically stacked low will be \nlocated over eastern Quebec, with broad troughing over the \nnortheastern CONUS. Farther west, midlevel ridging will be \ncentered over the western CONUS. Locally, eastern Kentucky will \nfind itself in a region of inconsequential quasi-zonal flow aloft\ninitially, although by Wednesday, troughing will deepen as an \nadditional closed upper level low pushes southeast through \nOntario. Flow aloft will briefly become more northwesterly before \nthe closed Canadian low lifts back northeast, with quasi zonal \nflow aloft persisting through the end of the work week. By the end\nof the forecast period, ridging does look to build in more from \nthe east. Perhaps more interestingly, there is some signal that a \ndisturbance may follow the western periphery of this ridge into \nKentucky this weekend, although uncertainty remains high this far \nout.\n\nAt the surface, high pressure over southwestern Pennsylvania will\nkeep winds easterly to east northeasterly on Tuesday, maintaining\na relatively dry air mass (with dewpoints generally in the 40s \nacross the forecast area). It will feel pleasant, too, with plenty\nof sunshine and highs ranging from the low to upper 70s roughly \nfrom the north to the south. Ideal conditions for radiational \ncooling will allow for a ridge valley split overnight, with valley\ntemperatures generally falling into the low 40s. Then, as the \nsurface high translates eastward, a new area of high pressure will\nbuild to the southwest near the Ozarks, with winds becoming more \nsouthwesterly by Wednesday. Highs will warm into the upper 70s and\nlow 80s, and dewpoints will also tick up a few degrees; even so, \nby the time that a cold front attempts to approach the area \nThursday, atmospheric moisture will be low enough to keep the \nfrontal passage dry. Behind the front, little change in sensible \nweather is expected, with the biggest pattern shift being a day or\nso of northerly winds before high pressure again builds in east \nof the forecast area and winds become more easterly. Regardless, \ntemperatures will still remain above average for this time of \nyear, with highs in the upper 70s to low 80s.\n\nThe next chance for rain looks to arrive this weekend, with the \nmost recent model guidance suggesting that a disturbance could \ntrack north towards Kentucky around this time, following the edge \nof the building ridge over the Atlantic Coast. Exact details are \nsparse at this point, but NBM output suggests chance PoPs by \nSunday, and this is corroborated by a signal for notable rain in \nensemble guidance; LREF QPF shows around a 50% chance of at least \nhalf an inch of rain in our southern counties on Sunday, around \nthe time that anomalous moisture returns to the area (with LREF \nmean PW values in the 70th to 80th percentile compared to \nclimatology). Of course, this comes with the caveat that these \nforecast details may change with time, especially this far out.",
